# program.md — autonomous CIFAR-10 recipe optimisation

## Task

Improve validation accuracy of the CIFAR-10 recipe in `train.py` by editing that
file and nothing else. Each of your proposals is trained for a fixed
**{{ train_seconds }} seconds of wall clock** and scored on a held-out 5 000-image
validation split. Higher is better.

## What the budget actually buys

Measured on this exact hardware with the unmodified file below:

| | |
|---|---|
| baseline validation accuracy | {{ baseline_val_acc }} |
| epochs completed in {{ train_seconds }} s | {{ baseline_epochs }} |
| optimiser steps completed | {{ baseline_steps }} |

This is a **short-budget regime**. A schedule written for hundreds of epochs will
never reach its useful range. Anything you propose is trained from scratch for
{{ train_seconds }} seconds -- there is no pretraining and no warm start.

## Rules

1. `train.py` is the only file you may change. `prepare_cifar.py` is off limits.
2. `TRAIN_SECONDS` must keep exactly the value it already has in the file.
   Changing it, or otherwise training for longer, invalidates the comparison and
   your proposal is rejected without being run. Your recipe is given roughly
   {{ train_seconds }} seconds of training per experiment.
3. Data comes only from `prepare_cifar.load_splits()`. The test split does not
   exist for you.
4. `build_model()` and `main()` must exist; `result.json` must carry
   `val_acc`, `epochs_completed`, `steps`, `train_seconds`, `peak_vram_mb`.
5. Allowed imports: torch, torchvision, numpy, math, time, json, random,
   argparse, pathlib, prepare_cifar. Nothing else.
6. Never ask for help or clarification. Propose, and keep proposing.

Everything else is yours: architecture, width, depth, normalisation, optimiser,
learning-rate schedule, batch size, augmentation, regularisation, mixed precision.

## Selection rule for this session

{% if patience == 1 -%}
**Greedy.** A proposal is kept only if it beats the best validation accuracy so far
by more than {{ eps }}. Anything else is reverted immediately and the next proposal
starts from the best recipe found so far.
{%- else -%}
**Patience {{ patience }}.** A proposal that does not beat the best validation
accuracy so far is kept provisionally, so that a change which pays off only in
combination with later changes gets a chance. After {{ patience }} consecutive
non-improving proposals the whole provisional chain is discarded and the recipe
returns to the best one found so far.
{%- endif %}

You have **{{ loop_budget }} experiments** in this session. Rejected and errored
proposals count against that budget, so proposals that do not parse or that break
the rules cost you real experiments.

